You are given the complete list of groups in one fictional universe at a
single fixed point in time, with their member lists.

List pairs of groups where {{ relation_desc }}.

Definition: judge the groups as entities, not their individual members.
Report a pair only when there is a concrete instance of the GROUPS acting
toward or against each other — a shared mission, a formal alliance, backing
or training or protecting one another as factions, a demonstrated clash of
goals, an ongoing conflict between the factions themselves — not just
members who happen to know or dislike each other personally.
Test: point to the specific thing the groups did (worked toward the same
goal, backed or supported one another, fought together, opposed each
other's aims, clashed as factions) — not a feeling, not reputation, not
"they seem like they would."
Include, for shared-goal / working-together pairs: groups on the same side
of a larger conflict; a group and the group it trains, protects, commands
or answers to, when that relationship is active in this period (mentorship
and hierarchy count — the pair need not be equals); groups that joined
forces for a specific mission or event.
Include, for opposed / against-each-other pairs: groups whose stated
purposes directly oppose each other (e.g. a hero organization and the
villain group it exists to stop); groups with a canonical ongoing rivalry
between them as institutions (not just a fair one-off competition); a
group formed in response to or in defense against the other.
ANTI: two groups simply existing in the same story with no demonstrated
interaction do not qualify. One member's personal friendship or grudge does
not make the groups themselves allied or rival — it must represent the
groups as wholes. Fair, structural competition (two classes in the same
tournament, two teams in the same league) is not rivalry by itself unless
there is a real antagonism between them beyond the event.
Everything not listed is undefined. Listing nothing is a legal answer, but
check every pair before concluding none qualify — a large group and a much
smaller one can still be allied or opposed even when very unequal in size.
If one group's members are entirely contained in another's, that is
membership, not a relation between two groups — skip it.
Use group names exactly as given.

Output JSON only:
{"pairs": [["<group>", "<group>"], ...]}
